---
import BaseLayout from '../../components/layout/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial Intelligence for performance marketing"
  description="Connect campaign response with customer, research and editorial evidence so performance and growth marketing improve organisational understanding as well as short-term results."
>
  <article class="container-site py-20">
    <div class="pm-inner">

      <header class="mb-16">
        <p class="t-label mb-6">Application</p>
        <h1 class="t-display mb-6">Editorial Intelligence for performance marketing</h1>
        <p class="pm-lead mb-8">Turn campaign response into knowledge that improves the next decision — not just the next ad.</p>
        <div class="prose-section mb-8">
          <p>Performance marketing produces more signals than most organisations know how to use: search queries, creative response, landing-page behaviour, conversion patterns and audience feedback.</p>
          <p>Yet much of that learning remains inside campaign dashboards. Teams optimise what is running without necessarily strengthening what the organisation understands about its customers, its market or the arguments that deserve further investment.</p>
          <p>Editorial Intelligence connects performance signals with research, customer evidence, sales feedback and editorial judgement. It helps teams interpret what the response might mean, carry useful learning beyond the platform and build a stronger evidence base for the next campaign.</p>
        </div>
        <a href="#working-model" class="pm-btn">Explore the working model ↓</a>
      </header>

      <hr class="divider mb-16" />

      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="pm-h2 mb-6">Performance data is a signal, not a strategy</h2>
        <div class="prose-section">
          <p>A dashboard can show which advert, audience, query or landing page produced a response. It cannot, by itself, explain the customer tension underneath that response or whether the learning should change the wider story.</p>
          <p>The usual answer is to make more versions of the winning asset. That may improve the campaign. It does not automatically improve the organisation's understanding.</p>
          <p>The larger opportunity is to treat campaign response as evidence: one source of behavioural signal that can be compared with what customers say, what sales teams hear, what research reveals and what the organisation already believes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-principle mt-8">
          <p class="t-label mb-3">The shift</p>
          <p>From optimising isolated assets to testing and strengthening the narrative behind them.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <hr class="divider mb-16" />

      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="pm-h2 mb-6">From editorial intelligence to growth</h2>
        <div class="prose-section mb-8">
          <p>Good growth marketing needs something worth testing.</p>
          <p>Editorial Intelligence works upstream of growth: finding the evidence, customer insight and expertise that can become a differentiated proposition, narrative or campaign.</p>
          <p>Growth marketing then returns evidence of its own. Which arguments attract attention? Which evidence builds confidence? Which messages lead people to act?</p>
        </div>

        <div class="pm-loop" aria-label="Evidence, narrative, activation, response, learning">
          <span>Evidence</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span>Narrative</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span>Activation</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span>Response</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span>Learning</span>
        </div>
        <p class="pm-loop-note mb-8">This is the <a href="/intelligence#operating-model">Editorial Intelligence operating model</a> applied to growth activity rather than a separate method. Signals become narrative, narrative is activated, and response returns as evidence for the next cycle.</p>

        <div class="prose-section">
          <p>Instead of simply producing more content and optimising its distribution, organisations can improve both what they communicate and what they learn from the response.</p>
          <p>Editorial Intelligence is not a replacement for growth marketing. It gives growth teams stronger material to work with and turns their results into evidence for what the organisation should say, investigate or develop next.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <hr class="divider mb-16" />

      <section id="working-model" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <p class="t-label mb-4">Working model</p>
        <h2 class="pm-h2 mb-8">A two-way learning system</h2>
        <p class="pm-intro mb-8">Editorial Intelligence creates a connection in both directions. Organisational evidence improves the campaign. Campaign response then returns new evidence to the organisation.</p>

        <div class="pm-flow" aria-label="Performance marketing learning system">
          <div class="pm-flow-step">
            <span class="pm-flow-num">01</span>
            <strong>Organisational evidence</strong>
            <span>Research, customer conversations, sales objections and market signals</span>
          </div>
          <span class="pm-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
          <div class="pm-flow-step">
            <span class="pm-flow-num">02</span>
            <strong>Narrative hypotheses</strong>
            <span>Specific tensions, claims and propositions worth testing</span>
          </div>
          <span class="pm-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
          <div class="pm-flow-step">
            <span class="pm-flow-num">03</span>
            <strong>Campaign activation</strong>
            <span>Creative, search, landing pages, audiences and offers</span>
          </div>
          <span class="pm-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
          <div class="pm-flow-step">
            <span class="pm-flow-num">04</span>
            <strong>Behavioural signals</strong>
            <span>Response, conversion, queries, drop-off and qualitative feedback</span>
          </div>
          <span class="pm-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
          <div class="pm-flow-step pm-flow-step--accent">
            <span class="pm-flow-num">05</span>
            <strong>Editorial synthesis</strong>
            <span>Patterns interpreted, assumptions challenged and learning returned to the evidence base</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <hr class="divider mb-16" />

      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="pm-h2 mb-8">Five moves in practice</h2>
        <div class="pm-moves">
          <div class="pm-move">
            <span class="pm-move-num">01</span>
            <div>
              <h3>Start with evidence</h3>
              <p>Bring together the customer, research, sales and market evidence already available before deciding what the campaign should say.</p>
              <span class="pm-output">Output: evidence map</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-move">
            <span class="pm-move-num">02</span>
            <div>
              <h3>Define the narrative hypotheses</h3>
              <p>Turn the evidence into a small number of precise propositions. State what each message assumes about the audience and what response would strengthen or challenge that assumption.</p>
              <span class="pm-output">Output: narrative test brief</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-move">
            <span class="pm-move-num">03</span>
            <div>
              <h3>Translate ideas into testable expressions</h3>
              <p>Create distinct creative, search and landing-page expressions without allowing format changes to blur what is actually being tested.</p>
              <span class="pm-output">Output: message and asset map</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-move">
            <span class="pm-move-num">04</span>
            <div>
              <h3>Interpret response in context</h3>
              <p>Compare platform performance with search language, customer comments, sales feedback and conversion quality. Separate a useful pattern from a temporary result.</p>
              <span class="pm-output">Output: campaign learning memo</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-move">
            <span class="pm-move-num">05</span>
            <div>
              <h3>Return learning to the system</h3>
              <p>Update the narrative, language bank, content priorities and evidence gaps so the next campaign begins from a stronger position.</p>
              <span class="pm-output">Output: reusable knowledge</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <hr class="divider mb-16" />

      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="pm-h2 mb-8">What changes</h2>
        <div class="pm-comparison" role="table" aria-label="Traditional performance marketing compared with an Editorial Intelligence approach">
          <div class="pm-comparison-row pm-comparison-head" role="row">
            <span role="columnheader">Campaign optimisation</span>
            <span role="columnheader">Editorial Intelligence</span>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-comparison-row" role="row">
            <span role="cell">Creative tests compare assets</span>
            <span role="cell">Creative tests examine narrative propositions</span>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-comparison-row" role="row">
            <span role="cell">Search terms optimise keyword coverage</span>
            <span role="cell">Search language reveals how demand is expressed</span>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-comparison-row" role="row">
            <span role="cell">Landing pages end the campaign journey</span>
            <span role="cell">Landing pages test evidence, language and argument</span>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-comparison-row" role="row">
            <span role="cell">Reports improve the next media flight</span>
            <span role="cell">Learning improves campaigns, content and positioning</span>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-comparison-row" role="row">
            <span role="cell">Campaign knowledge remains with the team or agency</span>
            <span role="cell">Knowledge becomes part of organisational memory</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <hr class="divider mb-16" />

      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="pm-h2 mb-8">The inputs and outputs</h2>
        <div class="pm-io-grid">
          <div class="pm-io-card">
            <p class="t-label mb-4">Useful inputs</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Customer and market research</li>
              <li>Sales objections and call evidence</li>
              <li>Search queries and audience language</li>
              <li>Creative and landing-page performance</li>
              <li>Conversion quality and funnel behaviour</li>
              <li>Customer comments and campaign feedback</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-io-card pm-io-card--accent">
            <p class="t-label mb-4">Reusable outputs</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Narrative hypothesis brief</li>
              <li>Message and creative test map</li>
              <li>Customer language bank</li>
              <li>Campaign learning memo</li>
              <li>Evidence gaps and research questions</li>
              <li>Updated editorial and campaign priorities</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <hr class="divider mb-16" />

      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="pm-h2 mb-6">What Editorial Intelligence does not replace</h2>
        <div class="prose-section mb-8">
          <p>Editorial Intelligence is not a substitute for performance or growth marketing expertise. It does not set bids, plan media spend, build attribution models, configure conversion tracking or manage channel operations. Nor does it run conversion-rate optimisation, lifecycle automation, marketing technology implementation or growth analytics.</p>
          <p>It works alongside those disciplines. Its job is to improve the evidence and narrative entering the campaign, interpret the signals coming out and prevent useful learning from disappearing when the campaign ends.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-boundary">
          <div>
            <p class="t-label mb-3">Performance and growth marketing own</p>
            <p>Media strategy, targeting, experiment delivery, conversion optimisation, measurement and marketing technology.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="t-label mb-3">Editorial Intelligence connects</p>
            <p>Evidence, narrative hypotheses, audience language, interpretation and organisational learning.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <hr class="divider mb-16" />

      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="pm-h2 mb-6">Where this is most useful</h2>
        <div class="prose-section mb-6">
          <p>This application is most relevant in complex B2B markets, where a click is only one small part of a longer decision and campaign response needs to be understood alongside trust, evidence and buyer-group dynamics.</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="pm-list">
          <li>Paid, editorial, research and product marketing teams are working from different versions of the story</li>
          <li>Campaign reports describe performance but do not influence wider positioning or content decisions</li>
          <li>The organisation has strong customer or research evidence that is not shaping creative strategy</li>
          <li>AI has made creative variation easier, but the team is less certain which ideas deserve testing</li>
          <li>Useful audience language and objections are repeatedly rediscovered rather than retained</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <hr class="divider mb-16" />

      <section class="mb-16">
        <p class="t-label mb-4">Status</p>
        <h2 class="pm-h2 mb-6">A working application to test</h2>
        <div class="prose-section">
          <p>This is an emerging application of Editorial Intelligence, not a settled performance marketing methodology.</p>
          <p>The core proposition is practical: campaign response becomes more valuable when it is interpreted with other evidence and retained as organisational knowledge. The next step is to test the model against real campaign programmes, examine which signals genuinely transfer beyond the platform and refine the working outputs.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <hr class="divider mb-16" />

      <section class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="pm-h2 mb-6">Start with the evidence you already have</h2>
        <div class="prose-section mb-8">
          <p>A Narrative Audit can review existing research, campaign material, customer insight and performance learning to identify the argument already present, the evidence supporting it and the gaps worth testing next.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-actions">
          <a href="/work/narrative-audit" class="pm-btn">Explore the Narrative Audit</a>
          <a href="/frameworks/editorial-activation" class="pm-link">See Editorial Activation →</a>
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .pm-inner { max-width: 42rem; }

  .prose-section { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.25rem; }
  .prose-section p,
  .pm-intro { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--color-ink-secondary); }

  .pm-lead {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 1.55rem);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    max-width: 34ch;
  }

  .pm-h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 3vw, 1.75rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .pm-btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
    border-radius: 4px;
    color: var(--color-surface);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: opacity 0.15s;
  }

  .pm-btn:hover { opacity: 0.88; }

  .pm-principle {
    padding: 1.5rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    background: var(--color-surface-secondary);
  }

  .pm-principle p:last-child {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .pm-loop {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem 0.65rem;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    border-radius: 6px;
    background: var(--color-surface-secondary);
  }

  .pm-loop span {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .pm-loop span:nth-child(even) { color: var(--color-accent); }

  .pm-loop-note {
    margin-top: 0.85rem;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-ink-tertiary);
  }

  .pm-loop-note a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 35%, transparent);
  }

  .pm-loop-note a:hover { border-color: var(--color-accent); }

  .pm-flow {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    border-radius: 6px;
    background: var(--color-surface-secondary);
    padding: 1rem;
  }

  .pm-flow-step {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2.25rem 1fr;
    column-gap: 0.75rem;
    row-gap: 0.25rem;
    padding: 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }

  .pm-flow-step--accent { border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 40%, var(--color-rule)); }
  .pm-flow-num { grid-row: 1 / 3; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--color-accent); padding-top: 0.2rem; }
  .pm-flow-step strong { font-size: 0.9375rem; font-weight: 500; color: var(--color-ink); }
  .pm-flow-step span:last-child { font-size: 0.8125rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--color-ink-secondary); }
  .pm-flow-arrow { align-self: center; color: var(--color-accent); padding: 0.35rem 0; font-size: 0.75rem; }

  .pm-moves { display: flex; flex-direction: column; border: 1px solid var(--color-rule); border-radius: 6px; overflow: hidden; }
  .pm-move { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 2.5rem 1fr; gap: 1rem; padding: 1.35rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule); background: var(--color-surface-secondary); }
  .pm-move:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .pm-move-num { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--color-accent); padding-top: 0.15rem; }
  .pm-move h3 { font-size: 0.9375rem; font-weight: 500; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 0.45rem; }
  .pm-move p { font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--color-ink-secondary); margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .pm-output { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.75rem; letter-spacing: 0.02em; color: var(--color-ink-tertiary); }

  .pm-comparison { border: 1px solid var(--color-rule); border-radius: 6px; overflow: hidden; }
  .pm-comparison-row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  .pm-comparison-row span { padding: 0.9rem 1rem; font-size: 0.8125rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--color-ink-secondary); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule); }
  .pm-comparison-row span + span { border-left: 1px solid var(--color-rule); color: var(--color-ink); background: var(--color-surface-secondary); }
  .pm-comparison-row:last-child span { border-bottom: none; }
  .pm-comparison-head span { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.75rem; letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-ink-tertiary); background: var(--color-surface-secondary); }
  .pm-comparison-head span + span { color: var(--color-accent); }

  .pm-io-grid,
  .pm-boundary { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1rem; }
  .pm-io-card,
  .pm-boundary > div { padding: 1.35rem; border: 1px solid var(--color-rule); border-radius: 6px; background: var(--color-surface-secondary); }
  .pm-io-card--accent { border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 35%, var(--color-rule)); }
  .pm-io-card ul,
  .pm-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.7rem; padding-left: 1.1rem; }
  .pm-io-card li,
  .pm-list li { font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--color-ink-secondary); }
  .pm-io-card li::marker,
  .pm-list li::marker { color: var(--color-accent); }
  .pm-boundary p:last-child { font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--color-ink-secondary); }

  .pm-actions { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 1.25rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .pm-link { font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-accent); text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 35%, transparent); padding-bottom: 1px; }
  .pm-link:hover { border-color: var(--color-accent); }

  @media (max-width: 639px) {
    .pm-io-grid,
    .pm-boundary { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .pm-comparison-row span { padding: 0.75rem; }
    .pm-move { grid-template-columns: 2rem 1fr; padding: 1.1rem; gap: 0.65rem; }
    .pm-flow { padding: 0.75rem; }
  }
</style>
